<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
    Nothing Phone (3) / Metroid - device HAL manifest (DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE).

    The stock dump now ships a VINTF fragment for almost every HAL the vendor
    image serves (vendor/etc/vintf/manifest/*.xml, 111 entries in
    proprietary-files.txt, plus the fragments the caf modules install from
    source). Two declarations of the same package/interface/instance are a
    conflict when libvintf merges the manifests, so this file declares only what
    nothing else declares:

      * gatekeeper and nfc: stock states them in its product manifest
        (vendor/etc/vintf/manifest_sun.xml and manifest_tuna.xml), which is a
        device manifest of its own, not a fragment, and is not shipped in the
        blob list. Our DEVICE_MANIFEST_FILE replaces it.
      * vendor.qti.hardware.spu: same, product manifest only (manifest_sun.xml).

    authsecret and vendor.qti.hardware.sensorscalibrate ship a binary and an
    init script but no fragment, and stock never starts them. Declaring them
    makes servicemanager wait on services nothing runs, which stalls boot.

    Everything else the device serves is covered by a shipped fragment and must
    not be repeated here. The check used to confirm that is described in
    README.md.

    The sepolicy version is left to assemble_vintf (BOARD_SEPOLICY_VERS), since
    the vendor policy is built from source.
-->
<manifest version="1.0" type="device" target-level="202404">

    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.gatekeeper</name>
        <fqname>IGatekeeper/default</fqname>
    </hal>

    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>android.hardware.nfc</name>
        <fqname>INfc/default</fqname>
    </hal>

    <hal format="aidl">
        <name>vendor.qti.hardware.spu</name>
        <version>2</version>
        <fqname>ISPUManager/default</fqname>
    </hal>

</manifest>
